  "description": "Simulate continuous iteration-level request batching, admitting new requests dynamically as completed sequences exit.\n\nContinuous batching eliminates idle GPU bubble latency caused by varying sequence lengths in static batching.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\text{Batch}_{t+1} = (\\text{Batch}_t \\setminus \\text{Completed}_t) \\cup \\text{Admitted}_t\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Track per-iteration generation steps for all active batch slots\n2. Evict completed sequences immediately upon generation of EOS token\n3. Admit pending requests from arrival queue into newly vacated batch slots\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'completed_requests' timestamps and total iterations.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Simulate Continuous Batching\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nRunning large AI models in production requires massive computing power. During text generation, memory bandwidth (fetching weights and past key-value caches from VRAM) is often the primary bottleneck rather than raw arithmetic compute. **Simulate Continuous Batching** optimizes hardware efficiency, memory consumption, or request serving throughput.\n\nContinuous batching eliminates idle GPU bubble latency caused by varying sequence lengths in static batching. In this problem, the goal is to: simulate continuous iteration-level request batching, admitting new requests dynamically as completed sequences exit.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Batch}_{t+1} = (\\text{Batch}_t \\setminus \\text{Completed}_t) \\cup \\text{Admitted}_t\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Track per-iteration generation steps for all active batch slots\n2. **Phase 2**: Evict completed sequences immediately upon generation of EOS token\n3. **Phase 3**: Admit pending requests from arrival queue into newly vacated batch slots\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Memory Bandwidth Bottleneck**: Quantizing weights to INT8 or INT4 cuts memory traffic in half or quarters, dramatically increasing token generation speeds.\n* **Serving Systems**: Dynamic batching and PagedAttention maximize GPU utilization by grouping requests arriving at different times.\n* **Latency vs. Throughput**: In production serving, systems engineers balance Time-To-First-Token (TTFT) against overall tokens-per-second throughput.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: requests = [{\"id\": 1, \"arrival_step\": 0, \"total_steps\": 2, \"steps_done\": 2}, {\"id\": 2, \"arrival_step\": 0, \"total_steps\": 4, \"steps_done\": 4}, {\"id\": 3, \"arrival_step\": 1, \"total_steps\": 2, \"steps_done\": 2}], max_batch_slots = 2\n\nOutput: {\"completed_requests\": [{\"id\": 1, \"finish_step\": 3}, {\"id\": 2, \"finish_step\": 5}, {\"id\": 3, \"finish_step\": 5}], \"total_steps_simulated\": 5}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated inference_continuous_batching under standard test conditions."
